title: "blinkrate: measurement rules and reported electric distribution reliability"
message: "If you use these findings or this code, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Morrissey
    given-names: Andrew
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8758-2687"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ASM-21/blinkrate"
url: "https://github.com/ASM-21/blinkrate"
version: 1.2.0
date-released: 2026-09-26
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - SAIDI
  - IEEE 1366
  - major event day
  - electric distribution reliability
  - EIA-861
abstract: >-
  IEEE 1366 permits utilities to exclude "major event days" from reported SAIDI
  using a threshold set 2.5 standard deviations up a log-normal fit to their own
  daily outage history. Using 6,245 utility-state-years of EIA-861 reliability
  filings from 2013-2018 and 2022, this work finds, directly from the filings,
  that between 40 and 57% of large investor-owned utilities change performance
  quartile depending on which of the two published SAIDI series is used to rank
  them, a reversal that survives stratification by storm exposure, and that six
  defensible rules for aggregating the same filings give national SAIDI spanning
  2.07x. It inverts the closed-form exclusion identity r = Phi(beta - sigma) to
  re-evaluate filed outage records at other thresholds: on the range of
  thresholds the literature supports, one to fifteen major event days a year,
  fleet SAIDI moves 1.52x to 3.16x depending on the daily-outage tail model. A
  comparison between the threshold's effect on one utility and the spread
  between utilities, reported in earlier versions, fails on every
  evidence-grounded threshold window and is withdrawn. Of all filings, 29.8%
  report no major event days; for large IEEE-standard filers the rate is
  consistent with genuine zero-event years, and the excess is concentrated among
  smaller utilities and self-defined-standard filers. A within-utility design
  covering 53 advanced-metering rollouts finds no detectable reliability effect
  in a design powered to detect 24%. A geographic placebo separates
  weather-driven spikes in the excluded portion from a slower methodological
  drift into using the exclusion at all.
